
<% content_for :title, "Who Am I Without It? | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="identity-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="identity-hero">

    <picture class="identity-background" aria-hidden="true">
      <source
        media="(min-width: 768px)"
        srcset="<%= asset_path('toolbox_who_am_i_without_it_desktop.webp') %>"
      >
      <%= image_tag "toolbox_who_am_i_without_it_mobile.webp",
          alt: "",
          loading: "eager" %>
    </picture>

    <div class="identity-hero-content">

      <p class="identity-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / TOOLBOX / WHO AM I WITHOUT IT?
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>WHO THE</span>
        <span>FUCK AM I</span>
        <span>WITHOUT IT?</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="identity-tagline">
        YOU CAN MISS IT<br>
        AND STILL LEAVE IT.
      </p>

      <p class="identity-intro">
        YOU DON'T HAVE TO HATE
        EVERYTHING ABOUT YOUR PAST
        TO STOP REPEATING IT.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / WHAT YOU MISS -->
  <section class="identity-section identity-pink">
    <div class="identity-inner">

      <p class="identity-number">01 / WHAT YOU MISS</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT DO YOU<br>
        ACTUALLY MISS?
      </h2>

      <div class="identity-copy">

        <p>
          You liked drinking.
          Sometimes it was genuinely
          fun. You liked the anticipation,
          the feeling of getting drunk,
          and the way an ordinary
          afternoon could become
          something different.
        </p>

        <p>
          Sometimes you drank
          because you were sad,
          in pain, or wanted
          to stop thinking.
          Other times you drank
          because you were already
          enjoying yourself and
          wanted to feel even better.
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't need to rewrite
          every memory as miserable
          simply because you're
          sober now.
        </p>

        <p>
          But what exactly are
          you missing? The alcohol?
          The feeling? The ritual?
          The people? Or a version
          of yourself you associate
          with being drunk?
        </p>

        <p>
          Those aren't necessarily
          the same thing.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="identity-grid">

        <div class="identity-box">
          <h3>THE FEELING.</h3>

          <p>
            Maybe you miss the
            pleasure, excitement,
            relaxation, or relief.
          </p>

          <p>
            Alcohol changed how
            you felt, and sometimes
            that was the entire point.
          </p>

          <p>
            What feeling are
            you remembering?
            What were you trying
            to experience or
            get away from?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="identity-box">
          <h3>THE RITUAL.</h3>

          <p>
            Buying alcohol,
            looking forward to
            drinking, settling
            into the evening,
            or marking the end
            of a difficult day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those routines became
            familiar. Without them,
            certain moments
            might feel strange.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sometimes you miss
            the anticipation as
            much as the experience.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="identity-box">
          <h3>THE PEOPLE &amp; BELONGING.</h3>

          <p>
            Certain friendships,
            conversations, celebrations,
            and experiences might
            be associated with alcohol.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some relationships still
            fit your life. Others
            might have depended
            on drinking together.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can appreciate
            those experiences
            without needing to
            recreate them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="identity-box">
          <h3>WHO YOU FELT LIKE.</h3>

          <p>
            Maybe you felt
            more spontaneous,
            outgoing, funny,
            adventurous, or
            less concerned
            about everything.
          </p>

          <p>
            But were those qualities
            created by alcohol,
            or did alcohol change
            how freely you
            expressed them?
          </p>

          <p>
            Don't automatically
            give drinking credit
            for your personality.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <div class="identity-callout">
        <p>
          WHEN YOU SAY YOU MISS
          DRINKING, WHAT PART
          ARE YOU ACTUALLY
          TALKING ABOUT?
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / THE WHOLE MEMORY -->
  <section class="identity-section identity-black">
    <div class="identity-inner">

      <p class="identity-number">02 / THE WHOLE MEMORY</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT DID<br>
        IT COST YOU?
      </h2>

      <div class="identity-copy">

        <p>
          The complicated thing
          about leaving alcohol
          behind is that it
          wasn't always unpleasant.
        </p>

        <p>
          There were good experiences.
          You enjoyed yourself.
          You experienced pleasure
          and relief that felt
          real at the time.
        </p>

        <p>
          But nostalgia has a
          way of leaving out
          the less enjoyable
          parts of the story.
        </p>

        <p>
          When you remember
          drinking, remember
          the entire experience.
          Not just the first
          drink or the feeling
          you were chasing.
        </p>

        <p>
          Remember what happened
          afterward, the decisions
          you made, and the
          consequences you had
          to live with.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can acknowledge
          what alcohol gave you
          without deciding that
          the overall arrangement
          was worth keeping.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="identity-grid">

        <div class="identity-box">
          <h3>WHAT IT GAVE ME.</h3>

          <p>
            Pleasure and excitement.
          </p>

          <p>
            Temporary relief
            from sadness,
            physical pain,
            or uncomfortable thoughts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Familiar rituals
            and something to
            look forward to.
          </p>

          <p>
            Moments of confidence,
            spontaneity, or
            social connection.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those experiences
            explain why drinking
            appealed to you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="identity-box">
          <h3>WHAT IT TOOK FROM ME.</h3>

          <p>
            Your judgment in
            situations where
            it mattered.
          </p>

          <p>
            Money, time,
            and opportunities.
          </p>

          <p>
            Safety, stability,
            and freedom.
          </p>

          <p>
            Relationships and
            other people's trust.
          </p>

          <p>
            Legal consequences
            and experiences
            you wouldn't choose
            to repeat.
          </p>

          <p>
            The relief was
            temporary. Some
            consequences weren't.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <div class="identity-callout">
        <p>
          YOU DON'T HAVE TO PRETEND
          IT WAS ALL BAD.
          YOU ALSO DON'T GET
          TO REMEMBER ONLY
          THE GOOD PARTS.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / WHAT BELONGS TO YOU -->
  <section class="identity-section identity-yellow">
    <div class="identity-inner">

      <p class="identity-number">03 / WHAT BELONGS TO YOU</p>

      <h2>
        WAS THAT IT?<br>
        OR WAS<br>
        SOME OF IT YOU?
      </h2>

      <div class="identity-copy">

        <p>
          It's easy to associate
          certain qualities with
          drinking simply because
          you experienced them
          while you were drunk.
        </p>

        <p>
          Maybe you remember being
          funny, outgoing, confident,
          creative, or spontaneous.
        </p>

        <p>
          Alcohol might have changed
          how you expressed those
          qualities, but it doesn't
          mean alcohol created them.
        </p>

        <p>
          You had a personality
          before you drank.
          You still have one now.
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't have to become
          a completely different
          person just because
          you're sober.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can still be
          sarcastic, outspoken,
          curious, unconventional,
          impatient, and funny.
        </p>

        <p>
          The question is which
          parts of your old
          experiences you'd like
          to carry forward,
          without carrying
          alcohol along with them.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="identity-grid">

        <div class="identity-box">
          <h3>WHAT I WANT TO KEEP.</h3>

          <p>
            Your humor,
            curiosity, creativity,
            and interest in
            new experiences.
          </p>

          <p>
            The friendships
            and activities
            that still make
            sense in your life.
          </p>

          <p>
            Your independence,
            individuality, and
            willingness to
            try unfamiliar things.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sobriety doesn't
            require you to
            abandon those qualities.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="identity-box">
          <h3>WHAT I WANT TO RECLAIM.</h3>

          <p>
            Enjoyment without
            intoxication.
          </p>

          <p>
            Confidence without
            losing your judgment.
          </p>

          <p>
            Relaxation without
            creating another problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spontaneity that doesn't
            compromise your safety
            or responsibilities.
          </p>

          <p>
            The freedom to
            have an experience
            without needing
            alcohol involved.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <div class="identity-callout">
        <p>
          WHAT HAVE YOU BEEN
          GIVING ALCOHOL CREDIT
          FOR THAT MIGHT HAVE
          BELONGED TO YOU
          ALL ALONG?
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / GETTING TO KNOW YOURSELF -->
  <section class="identity-section identity-purple">
    <div class="identity-inner">

      <p class="identity-number">04 / GETTING TO KNOW YOURSELF</p>

      <h2>
        WHO ARE YOU<br>
        GETTING TO<br>
        KNOW NOW?
      </h2>

      <div class="identity-copy">

        <p>
          You don't need to
          reinvent your entire
          personality because
          you're no longer drinking.
        </p>

        <p>
          But there might be
          things you haven't
          spent much time
          experiencing sober.
        </p>

        <p>
          Certain situations
          might feel unfamiliar.
          Activities you associated
          with drinking might
          feel different.
          You might discover
          that some things
          still interest you
          and others don't.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can explore that
          without expecting every
          experience to become
          a revelation.
        </p>

        <p>
          Some things will
          be fun. Some will
          be boring. Some
          will be awkward.
        </p>

        <p>
          You don't need to
          turn sobriety into
          a complete personality
          makeover.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can simply
          continue getting
          to know yourself.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="identity-grid">

        <div class="identity-box">
          <h3>WHAT I LIKE.</h3>

          <p>
            Making things,
            engraving glass,
            drawing, designing,
            and experimenting
            with new materials.
          </p>

          <p>
            Music, humor,
            spirituality, learning,
            and whatever else
            catches your interest.
          </p>

          <p>
            These activities
            don't have to
            become recovery
            exercises.
          </p>

          <p>
            They're simply
            things you enjoy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="identity-box">
          <h3>WHAT I VALUE.</h3>

          <p>
            Independence,
            honesty, creativity,
            and the ability
            to make your
            own decisions.
          </p>

          <p>
            Relationships built
            on trust and
            mutual respect.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stability that
            allows you to
            pursue your interests
            without repeatedly
            dealing with
            unnecessary consequences.
          </p>

          <p>
            Consider what
            actually matters
            to you now.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="identity-box">
          <h3>WHAT I NEED.</h3>

          <p>
            Enjoyment, connection,
            novelty, relaxation,
            and opportunities
            to feel like
            yourself.
          </p>

          <p>
            Room to be alone
            when you want
            solitude and
            support when
            you need it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ways to manage
            pain, difficult moods,
            and ordinary stress
            that don't involve
            alcohol.
          </p>

          <p>
            Your needs didn't
            disappear when
            you stopped drinking.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="identity-box">
          <h3>WHAT I'M WILLING TO TRY.</h3>

          <p>
            Revisit an activity
            you used to associate
            with drinking,
            if it's appropriate
            for your recovery.
          </p>

          <p>
            Try a new craft,
            spend time with
            someone, or build
            a different routine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Experiment without
            expecting every
            experience to
            be wonderful.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can discover
            what works by
            actually trying things.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 / ROOM FOR GRIEF -->
  <section class="identity-section identity-black">
    <div class="identity-inner">

      <p class="identity-number">05 / ROOM FOR GRIEF</p>

      <h2>
        YOU CAN MISS IT.<br>
        YOU CAN STILL<br>
        LEAVE IT.
      </h2>

      <div class="identity-copy">

        <p>
          You can grieve a
          familiar way of living,
          even when that way
          of living caused
          considerable harm.
        </p>

        <p>
          You might miss certain
          rituals, experiences,
          friendships, or feelings
          you associate
          with drinking.
        </p>

        <p>
          You might even miss
          a version of yourself
          who seemed more carefree
          or less concerned
          about consequences.
        </p>

        <p>
          Missing those things
          doesn't mean you
          want to return
          to everything
          that came with them.
        </p>

        <p>
          And you don't have
          to hate alcohol
          to recognize that
          it no longer belongs
          in your life.
        </p>

        <p>
          Sometimes an evening
          feels different without
          drinking. Sometimes
          you're bored, sad,
          or uncomfortable.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can let those
          experiences be different
          without immediately
          trying to replace
          what you lost.
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="identity-grid">

        <div class="identity-box">
          <h3>WHAT I'M GRIEVING.</h3>

          <p>
            The pleasure
            and anticipation.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rituals that
            once marked certain
            parts of your day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some relationships
            and social experiences.
          </p>

          <p>
            The feeling of
            being temporarily
            free from pain,
            sadness, or
            constant thinking.
          </p>

          <p>
            You're allowed to
            acknowledge those losses
            without romanticizing
            the consequences.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="identity-box">
          <h3>WHAT HELPS ME KEEP GOING.</h3>

          <p>
            Remembering the
            whole experience,
            not just the
            enjoyable beginning.
          </p>

          <p>
            Making room for
            interests and
            relationships that
            don't involve alcohol.
          </p>

          <p>
            Allowing uncomfortable
            feelings without
            needing to immediately
            change them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Using your relapse
            plan and recovery
            support when nostalgia
            begins turning into
            a desire to drink.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <div class="identity-callout">
        <p>
          "I CAN MISS HOW IT FELT
          WITHOUT WANTING EVERYTHING
          THAT CAME WITH IT.
          I DON'T HAVE TO GO BACK
          JUST BECAUSE I REMEMBER
          SOMETHING I ENJOYED."
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 / STILL BECOMING -->
  <section class="identity-section identity-pink">
    <div class="identity-inner">

      <p class="identity-number">06 / STILL BECOMING</p>

      <h2>
        YOU DON'T NEED<br>
        A FINISHED<br>
        FUCKING ANSWER.
      </h2>

      <div class="identity-copy">

        <p>
          You don't need
          to discover an
          entirely new personality
          now that you're sober.
        </p>

        <p>
          You're still the
          person who enjoys
          making things, laughing
          at ridiculous ideas,
          learning something new,
          and following whatever
          interests you.
        </p>

        <p>
          You're also someone
          whose life includes
          consequences from drinking.
          Those experiences matter,
          and taking responsibility
          for them matters.
        </p>

        <p>
          But they don't have
          to become the only
          way you understand
          yourself.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can change your
          routines, develop new
          interests, reconsider
          relationships, and decide
          what you want to
          carry forward.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can also keep
          parts of your old
          life that never
          needed to change.
        </p>

        <p>
          Not every experience
          needs to become
          a lesson about
          addiction or recovery.
          You can just
          have a life.
        </p>

        <p>
          And you don't have
          to figure out
          exactly who you're
          becoming before
          you're allowed
          to enjoy it.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can appreciate
          what alcohol once
          seemed to offer,
          recognize what it
          eventually cost,
          and decide that
          the arrangement
          no longer works
          for you.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can miss parts
          of your past without
          needing to recreate them.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>
            YOU'RE STILL YOU.
            THERE'S JUST MORE
            TO FIND OUT.
          </strong>
        </p>

      </div>

      <div class="identity-callout">
        <p>
          WHAT PARTS OF YOURSELF
          DO YOU WANT TO KEEP?
          WHAT ARE YOU READY
          TO LEAVE BEHIND?
          AND WHAT ARE YOU STILL
          CURIOUS TO DISCOVER?
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

</main>
